Below is a description of the scenario, the root cause, and a single *SEARCH/REPLACE* patch that will allow unmanaged layers (like the select‐overlay) to respect their declared zIndex rather than always being forced to the very top.

1) Bug scenario & root cause  
   - When you use `ol.interaction.Select`, OpenLayers creates an *unmanaged* vector layer internally for highlighting the selected features.  
   - In `Layer#setMap`, that unmanaged layer is installed with a `PRECOMPOSE` listener which unconditionally sets  
       `layerState.managed = false;`  
       `layerState.zIndex = Infinity;`  
     forcing it to render above absolutely everything else.  
   - As a result, **no matter** what `zIndex` you give your own layers (or the “hover” red–dot layer in your example), the select overlay is always on top.

2) Fix  
   Remove the `layerState.zIndex = Infinity` override so that the unmanaged layer will pick up whatever zIndex the layer itself has been assigned (defaulting to 0).  If you want your select‐overlay underneath something, you can now explicitly give it a lower zIndex.

